{"conjecture": "Long-running systems accumulate a growing gap between their statically present code and their dynamically reachable behavior because each locally correct change optimizes for not breaking existing paths, not for pruning newly unreachable ones. The dead paths remain syntactically present and type-checkable, so they continue to participate in the developer's mental model of what the system 'can do,' forcing every future change to reason about behavioral possibilities that can never actually execute. The system becomes harder to change not because the live behavior grows more complex, but because the ratio of unreachable-to-reachable code increases monotonically, and no individual change has sufficient scope to reverse it."}
